\title{Cognition as Braiding: The Writhe as a Well-Defined Invariant of Cognitive Braids}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-04}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a topological model of cognition in which a cognitive process is
identified with an element of the Artin braid group $B_n$, where $n$ counts the
participating brain regions and the interleaving of neural firing sequences is
modeled by the crossing of braid strands. Two cognitive processes are declared
\emph{cognitively equivalent} when their braid words are related by the Artin
relations---distant-generator commutation and the braid relation---interpreted
as the topological analogue of Reidemeister moves. Within this framework, any
well-defined measure of thought must be invariant under cognitive equivalence.
We isolate and rigorously establish the simplest such invariant, the
\emph{writhe} (signed crossing number), proving both a word-combinatorial
invariance theorem and its group-theoretic strengthening: the writhe descends to
a genuine group homomorphism $B_n \to \Z$. The argument is non-circular---the
writhe is defined purely as a sum over letters, before the equivalence relation
is introduced---and the descent is obtained through the universal property of a
presented group. We situate the result within the broader conjectural program in
which the Jones polynomial and its quantum dimension distinguish trivial,
creative (trefoil), and confused (figure-eight) thoughts, and discuss why the
writhe, an abelian invariant, is necessary but not sufficient for that finer
classification.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The connectome is a network, but cognition is not a static graph---it is a
\emph{process} unfolding in time. When a thought forms, brain regions activate
in sequences that overlap, hand off, and revisit one another. Drawing the
timeline of each participating region and recording how these timelines pass
over and under one another yields, naturally, a braid.

This paper takes that picture seriously and asks a foundational question: which
numerical measures of a thought are well defined? A measure that changes when we
merely retell the same thought in a different order is measuring an artifact of
the telling, not the thought. The right notion of ``same thought'' is
topological, and the right measures are topological invariants.

Our contributions are: (1) a precise formalization of cognitive processes as
braid words over signed Artin generators and of cognitive equivalence as a
relation generated by the Artin moves in arbitrary context; (2) a rigorous proof
that the writhe is invariant under cognitive equivalence; (3) the
group-theoretic strengthening that the writhe descends to a homomorphism
$B_n \to \Z$; and (4) a discussion locating this result within the conjectural
Jones-polynomial program for cognition.

\section{Background: braid groups and topological equivalence}

The Artin braid group $B_n$ on $n$ strands is generated by elementary crossings $\sigma_1,\dots,\sigma_{n-1}$, where $\sigma_i$ passes strand $i$ over strand $i+1$; composition is vertical stacking of braid diagrams. Artin's presentation imposes exactly two families of relations, $\sigma_i\sigma_j = \sigma_j\sigma_i$ for $|i-j|\ge 2$ and $\sigma_i\sigma_{i+1}\sigma_i = \sigma_{i+1}\sigma_i\sigma_{i+1}$, the second being the non-abelian braid relation. Closing a braid (joining top to bottom endpoints) yields a knot or link; by Alexander's theorem every knot so arises, and Markov's theorem characterizes when two closures are isotopic. Our cognitive equivalence on braid \emph{words}---the Artin relations applied in context---is the intrinsic pre-closure analogue of the Reidemeister moves, capturing the rearrangements of a firing sequence that preserve the underlying tangle. A braid \emph{invariant} is a quantity constant on equivalence classes; the abelianization $B_n\to B_n^{\mathrm{ab}}\cong\Z$ (every generator to $1$) is the simplest, and its value is the writhe.

\section{Braid words as cognitive processes}

Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$, the number of brain regions.

\begin{definition}[Letter]
A \emph{letter} is a pair $\ell = (i, s)$ with $i \in \{0,\dots,n-1\}$ a
generator index and $s \in \{\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false}\}$ a sign. The letter
$(i,\mathrm{true})$ denotes the positive Artin generator $\sigma_i$, and
$(i,\mathrm{false})$ denotes its inverse $\sigma_i^{-1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Braid word]
A \emph{braid word} is a finite list of letters $w = [\ell_1,\dots,\ell_k]$,
representing the cognitive process in which the elementary crossings occur in
that temporal order.
\end{definition}

Intuitively, $\sigma_i$ is a single directed firing event in which region $i$
leads its neighbor $i+1$; $\sigma_i^{-1}$ is the same event reversed. A thought
is a scripted sequence of such events.

\section{The writhe}

\begin{definition}[Writhe]
The writhe of a letter is $\writhe(i,s) = +1$ if $s = \mathrm{true}$ and $-1$ if
$s = \mathrm{false}$. The writhe of a word $w = [\ell_1,\dots,\ell_k]$ is
\[
  \writhe(w) = \sum_{m=1}^{k} \writhe(\ell_m).
\]
\end{definition}

Equivalently $\writhe(w)$ is the number of positive crossings minus the number
of negative crossings---the \emph{net directed charge} of the thought.

\begin{proposition}[Additivity]\label{prop:add}
For all braid words $u,v$, $\writhe(u \mathbin{+\!\!+} v) = \writhe(u) + \writhe(v)$,
where $\mathbin{+\!\!+}$ denotes concatenation.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The defining sum over the letters of a concatenation splits into the sum over
$u$ plus the sum over $v$.
\end{proof}

\section{Cognitive equivalence and invariance}

\begin{definition}[Cognitive equivalence]
Braid equivalence $\sim$ is the smallest equivalence relation on braid words
closed under application in arbitrary context $p \mathbin{+\!\!+} \cdot \mathbin{+\!\!+} q$ of:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Distant commutation.} For indices $i,j$ with $i+1<j$ and arbitrary
signs $s,t$,
\[ p \mathbin{+\!\!+} [(i,s),(j,t)] \mathbin{+\!\!+} q \;\sim\; p \mathbin{+\!\!+} [(j,t),(i,s)] \mathbin{+\!\!+} q. \]
\item \textbf{Braid relation.} For indices $i,j$ with $j=i+1$,
\[ p \mathbin{+\!\!+} [(i,\top),(j,\top),(i,\top)] \mathbin{+\!\!+} q \;\sim\;
   p \mathbin{+\!\!+} [(j,\top),(i,\top),(j,\top)] \mathbin{+\!\!+} q, \]
where $\top=\mathrm{true}$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Writhe-preserving substitution]\label{lem:subst}
If $\writhe(s) = \writhe(t)$, then
$\writhe(p \mathbin{+\!\!+} s \mathbin{+\!\!+} q) = \writhe(p \mathbin{+\!\!+} t \mathbin{+\!\!+} q)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:add} both sides equal
$\writhe(p) + \writhe(s) + \writhe(q)$ and $\writhe(p)+\writhe(t)+\writhe(q)$,
which agree since $\writhe(s)=\writhe(t)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Writhe is a cognitive invariant]\label{thm:inv}
If $u \sim v$ then $\writhe(u) = \writhe(v)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on the derivation of $u \sim v$. Reflexivity, symmetry, and
transitivity are immediate since integer equality is an equivalence relation.
For distant commutation, apply Lemma~\ref{lem:subst} with the two-letter
subwords $[(i,s),(j,t)]$ and $[(j,t),(i,s)]$; both have writhe
$\writhe(i,s)+\writhe(j,t)$ by commutativity of addition. For the braid
relation, apply Lemma~\ref{lem:subst} with the three-letter subwords
$[(i,\top),(j,\top),(i,\top)]$ and $[(j,\top),(i,\top),(j,\top)]$, each of writhe
$+3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Non-circularity]
The writhe is defined as a sum over the letters of a word, without any reference
to $\sim$. Hence Theorem~\ref{thm:inv} is a genuine theorem, not a definitional
artifact: it is a well-defined braid invariant without circular dependencies.
\end{remark}

\section{Worked examples}

\emph{Linear reasoning (trivial thought):} $\sigma_0\sigma_0^{-1}$ has writhe $1+(-1)=0$ and is equivalent to the empty word. \emph{Creative insight (trefoil):} $\sigma_0\sigma_1\sigma_0$ has writhe $+3$; its closure is the trefoil, and by the braid relation it equals $\sigma_1\sigma_0\sigma_1$, also of writhe $+3$, yet it cannot be combed straight. \emph{Confused thought (figure-eight):} $\sigma_0\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma_0\sigma_1^{-1}$ has writhe $1-1+1-1=0$; the tangle is genuinely knotted but its net directed charge---and, as the next section shows, its information content---vanishes.

\section{Descent to the braid group}

\begin{definition}[Artin relators]
Let $F_n$ be the free group on generators $x_0,\dots,x_{n-1}$, with $x_i$
written $\of(i)$. The set $R_n \subseteq F_n$ of braid relators consists of the
far-commutation commutators $x_i x_j x_i^{-1} x_j^{-1}$ for $i+1<j$ and the
braid relators $x_i x_j x_i (x_j x_i x_j)^{-1}$ for $j=i+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Braid group]
$B_n = \langle x_0,\dots,x_{n-1} \mid R_n\rangle = F_n / \langle\!\langle R_n
\rangle\!\rangle$, with canonical map $\of$ sending each index to its generator
class.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Writhe homomorphism]
Let $w\colon F_n \to \Z$ be the unique homomorphism with $w(x_i)=+1$ for all $i$;
automatically $w(x_i^{-1}) = -1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Descent]\label{thm:descent}
The homomorphism $w$ vanishes on every relator in $R_n$ and therefore descends
to a well-defined homomorphism $\overline{w}\colon B_n \to \Z$ with
$\overline{w}(\of(i)) = +1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We have $w(x_i x_j x_i^{-1} x_j^{-1}) = 1+1-1-1 = 0$ and
$w(x_i x_j x_i (x_j x_i x_j)^{-1}) = 3-3 = 0$. Thus $w$ kills all relators, and
by the universal property of the presented group $B_n$, it factors uniquely
through the quotient to give $\overline{w}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The writhe is a homomorphism $B_n \to \Z$, independent of the word
representative, so the net directed charge is an intrinsic feature of a
cognitive process.
\end{corollary}

\section{Interpretation and the Jones-polynomial program}

Within the cognitive model: a \emph{trivial thought} (linear reasoning) is a
combable braid of small writhe; a \emph{creative insight} is a genuinely knotted
braid, archetypically the trefoil, with standard Jones polynomial
$V(t) = -t^{-4} + t^{-3} + t^{-1}$; a \emph{confused thought} is the figure-eight
knot. The information content of a thought is $I = \log|V(e^{i\pi/3})|$, the
logarithm of a quantum dimension (there $|V| = (\sqrt 3)^d$).
Under this measure the trivial thought yields $I=0$, the trefoil the positive
value $\tfrac12\log 3$, and the figure-eight collapses to $I=0$: confusion is
informationally indistinguishable from thinking nothing.

The writhe is the abelian invariant underneath this program---the part of a
braid's topology surviving passage to $B_n^{\mathrm{ab}}\cong\Z$. It provides a
robust, provably well-defined handle on one coordinate of a thought, and yields
the immediate lower bound that a process of net charge $c$ contains at least
$|c|$ crossings. But because it sees only net charge, it cannot distinguish the
trefoil from a straightened braid of equal charge; the non-abelian Jones
invariant is required for the finer classification. The writhe thus serves as a
rigorously secured base camp for the theory of thought topology.

\section{Discussion and future work}

We have established with full rigor that the net directed charge of a cognitive
braid is a well-defined invariant, both at the level of words under cognitive
equivalence and at the level of the braid group as a homomorphism to $\Z$. This
is the abelian foundation of a larger conjectural program in which the
non-abelian Jones polynomial and its quantum dimension distinguish trivial,
creative, and confused thoughts. Concrete extensions include modeling a train of
thought as braiding of Fibonacci anyons (creative capacity growing at rate
$\log\varphi$), reading a thought's visibility off the three-torsion of the
double branched cover of the associated knot, and combining the writhe with a
non-abelian swap-cost into a two-invariant firing-cost bound. The overarching
thesis is as striking as it is testable: thinking is braiding, the topology of a
thought measures its quality, and creative insights are literally knotted.

